# A recursion nothing can see past.
#
# Every way through this body calls the function again, so no trace ever
# reaches a value, and there is nothing for a second trace to be checked
# against. The checker says which function it could not work out and
# what would fix it, rather than looping or guessing.
#
# The `+ 1` is what keeps this about the checker: it puts the call in an
# operand rather than in tail position, so src/optimization.py leaves it
# alone. Written `forever(n + 1)` on its own, the recursion would become
# a loop before the checker ever saw it (see docs/optimization.md), and
# a function that never returns is not the same question as one whose
# return type cannot be worked out.
#
# This program is expected to FAIL type checking. Its `.ast.out` holds
# the error message, not a program's output.

def forever(n):
    forever(n + 1) + 1

print(forever(1))
